# 19. Lessons learned today

### Lesson 1 — inspect every pipeline boundary

Looking only at the final KG was insufficient.

The useful debugging sequence was:

```text
MinerU output
↓
parse cache
↓
LightRAG chunks
↓
LLM raw extraction
↓
KG
```

Each layer answers a different question.

### Lesson 2 — parsing and KG extraction are separate problems

MinerU had already extracted the table extremely well.

The original failure occurred later:

```text
MinerU            ✅
RAG-Anything      ⚠
LightRAG chunk    ❌
KG                ❌
```

Therefore replacing MinerU or introducing a VLM would have attacked the wrong problem.

### Lesson 3 — don't tune prompts before proving the LLM received the data

Initially the table values were missing from the KG.

The correct question was not:

```text
"Why didn't the LLM understand 2.4 kW?"
```

but:

```text
"Did the LLM ever receive 2.4 kW?"
```

It had not.

### Lesson 4 — generic KG extraction is insufficient for HAZOP

General semantic extraction prioritizes concepts such as:

```text
Hydrogen
Enapter
Wi-Fi
CE Mark
```

HAZOP needs explicit operational facts such as:

```text
Output Pressure
Water Input Pressure Range
Temperature Range
Production Rate
Power Consumption
```

These need their own extraction schema.

### Lesson 5 — numerical specifications should be first-class engineering knowledge

Instead of:

```text
EL 4.0
description:
"500 NL/h, 35 barg, 42 kg..."
```

the better graph is:

```text
EL 4.0
 ├─ has_parameter → Production Rate
 ├─ has_limit     → Output Pressure
 ├─ has_parameter → Operative Power Consumption
 └─ has_limit     → Water Input Pressure Range
```

### Lesson 6 — provenance and engineering semantics are different

```text
belongs_to(Table)
```

means where a fact came from.

```text
has_parameter
```

means what the fact means engineering-wise.

Both are useful, but they serve different purposes.

### Lesson 7 — same identity should normally be merged

```text
EL40
EL 4.0
AEM Electrolyser EL 4.0
```

should normally become one canonical equipment entity, not three nodes connected with `is`.

### Lesson 8 — raw LLM cache is critical for debugging

The KG only showed:

```text
18 Ent + 0 Rel
```

which initially looked like a relationship-extraction problem.

The raw cache revealed the actual root cause:

```text
response truncation
```

That changed the diagnosis completely.

### Lesson 9 — changing one variable at a time worked

The table-only test kept:

```text
same table
same LLM
same embedding model
same LightRAG
```

and changed individual variables.

That allowed us to distinguish:

```text
ingestion problem
vs
prompt problem
vs
generation-length problem
```

